Windows, displays, drinking glasses, or lasers &ndash; glass is everywhere. Reason enough for the UN to declare 2022 the Year of Glass worldwide to honor the scientific, economic, and cultural significance of this unique material. Heraeus has also focused on glass for more than 120 years. Today, several of our Global Business Units are driving the expansion of its applications in many fields.

Shooting a Bullet into Liquid Steel: The Falcon Project&nbsp;
The story of Falcon&nbsp;began when&nbsp;Product Development Expert&nbsp;Gert-Jan Bex and&nbsp;IP Manager&nbsp;Guido&nbsp;Neyens&nbsp;from Heraeus Electro-Nite in Belgium&nbsp;decided to take&nbsp;initiative: &ldquo;If we can measure temperature&nbsp;safely&nbsp;and fast&nbsp;inside a furnace filled with liquid steel, we should be able to measure oxygen&nbsp;as well&rdquo;,&nbsp;Gert-Jan&nbsp;thought. After all, customers kept asking for&nbsp;exactly this kind of solution.
Why? Because in steelmaking, oxygen changes everything. It affects quality, yield, energy consumption, and process stability. Yet for decades, measuring it remained slow, dangerous, and partly dependent on estimation. &nbsp;
From the Lab into the Steel
The Cell
To measure oxygen, the team first had to create an oxygen cell. Together with the plasma spray* operator Ouchan Baghdadi it took only few weeks to produce a cell with a response time of just over one second.&nbsp;
*Plasma spraying is a thermal coating process in which powdered metals or ceramics are melted in a hot plasma arc and projected at high speed onto a component. It is used to make surfaces extremely resistant to wear, corrosion, and heat.&nbsp; 
The Project
The project moved along quickly. &ldquo;We already had a fast oxygen cell&rdquo;, Guido recalls. &ldquo;The real challenge was figuring out how to get it safely into the steel.&rdquo; To get an outside perspective, they brought in an external engineering group to run brainstorming sessions with additional specialists. The discussions produced ideas ranging from practical to wildly ambitious: &ldquo;Someone suggested we shoot a swarm of drones inside the furnace&rdquo;, Gert-Jan laughs. &ldquo;We wanted to keep our minds as open as possible.&rdquo; In the end, the process clarified the initial direction: launching a probe toward the steel while cables carry the signal back out.
The Bullet
&ldquo;We called our measuring head &lsquo;the bullet&rsquo; because it literally shoots the fast oxygen cell into the furnace&rdquo;, Guido explains. &ldquo;But designing it turned out to be far more delicate than expected.&rdquo; The probe needed to be compact enough to move quickly through the process, yet heavy and stable enough not to float on the surface of the molten steel. After multiple iterations, the team settled on copper as the core material. &ldquo;Its high density gave the probe enough weight, while also allowing it to withstand the brutal conditions long enough to capture a reliable signal&rdquo;, Product Engineer Yusuf Demirel explains.&nbsp;
The Launch
With the bullet design taking shape, the next challenge was figuring out how to launch it into the furnace reliably and with as little mechanical complexity as possible. &ldquo;We decided to use compressed air to avoid unnecessary moving parts in the system&rdquo;, Technician Designer Frank Stevens says. He developed a compact clamping mechanism that held the probe securely in place until launch: &ldquo;The idea was simple: As the probe moves into a cone-shaped opening, the clamp releases automatically, allowing the system to pull the probe in and accelerate it toward the steel using only air pressure.&rdquo;&nbsp;
As the team kept solving the technical quests, the true challenge remained: Will the probe actually work in a real electric arc furnace with real, hot liquid steel? &nbsp;
The Challenge 
Old Method at the Open Furnace
Traditional measurements often require interrupting the process by opening the furnace and exposing workers to extreme harsh conditions when manual measuring. Therefore, only single oxygen measurements were applied, giving only limited insight into the process.&nbsp;
The Core Idea around the Probe
The solution that Falcon makes possible: launch a probe into liquid steel through the side wall of the furnace, capture the signal, and retrieve the data before the environment destroys the sensor.&nbsp;
From Estimation to Precision due to Data Signals
Being able to perfom multiple oxygen measurements gives operators real-time process insights for better efficiency and quality.
&nbsp;
Turning Imagination into Innovation
Instead of building an elaborate system around the furnace, the team landed on the counterintuitive solution: &ldquo;We decided to send the sensor directly into the process, capture the measurement immediately, and get back out before the conditions destroyed it&rdquo;, Gert-Jan says.&nbsp;
Launching a probe through open air was one thing; firing it into liquid steel was another entirely. In January 2023, the team tested the concept in liquid steel conditions in their own induction furnace for the first time.&nbsp;
&ldquo;I remember all of us starring at screen waiting for a measurement signal to appear&rdquo;, Frank recalls. When the first signal appeared, the concept stopped being theoretical. &nbsp;
&ldquo;The next question was whether it could work under real production conditions&rdquo;, Technician Equipment Engineer Martijn Vromans says. &ldquo;We drove to Paris for our first customer visit, and they were immediately interested. Together, we developed an installation concept that would use a small existing furnace opening as the entry point.&rdquo;&nbsp;
Pressure, Fire, and Persistence
The months that followed were demanding in ways that had little to do with presentation slides or innovation language. &nbsp;
&ldquo;Our tests required precise coordination between multiple people operating valves and timing sequences manually&rdquo;, Martijn remembers. &ldquo;Probes were still being refined one by one. Components had to survive temperatures and conditions that destroyed equipment routinely.&rdquo;&nbsp;
At one stage, the project suffered a major setback when a fire damaged parts of an installed system and destroyed critical hardware. The delay cost months of work.&nbsp;
What mattered afterward, several team members say, was less the technical recovery than the reaction itself. &ldquo;There was never a moment where people wanted to quit,&rdquo; Technical Designer Lode Vansteenwegen says. &ldquo;Everyone just kept moving.&rdquo; The team rebuilt the damaged systems, continued testing at other sites, and kept refining the design under real production conditions.&nbsp;
During that period, relationships with customers also changed noticeably. &ldquo;Steel plants that had initially approached our technology cautiously began adapting their own infrastructure around it&rdquo;, Gert-Jan recalls proudly. Entry ports were modified, air lines rerouted, installation spaces rebuilt. Operators who had once observed the tests from a distance increasingly treated the system as part of their everyday process.&nbsp;
By 2024, Falcon had evolved from a fragile prototype into a working production system.&nbsp;
From Prototype to Production
Real Production Use
By 2025, operators were using the system as part of daily furnace operations.
Customer Commitment
Some customers modified air lines, roofs, and furnace setups to integrate the system permanently.
Built Together
Training and close collaboration with operators became a key part of scaling the technology.
Proud as a Falcon 
By 2026, the project had earned recognition inside and outside the company. But more importantly, it reflected the way the team itself had worked: trusting each other, adapting quickly, and continuing forward even when setbacks occurred.
In the end, Falcon became more than a measurement system. It became a story about people building something nobody was sure could be done.
